  TDMI_gsrglobal_PCB     FD_PCB                   -0.111   +0.008  0.0434  0/14

## F. TR-level FD versus DVARS (raw `ts`), matched vs mismatched
  DMT: diag mean +0.045 (min -0.104), off-diag mean -0.023 (max +0.228); argmax==self 4/14
/home/vilalius/dmt-phiid/scripts/10_subject_alignment_check.py:254: RuntimeWarning: Mean of empty slice
  D.append(np.sqrt(np.nanmean(d ** 2, 0)))
  PCB: diag mean +0.060 (min -0.160), off-diag mean +0.006 (max +0.195); argmax==self 4/14
  Reading: the distributed timeseries are denoised and band-passed (motion regressors, scrubbing with
  interpolation per the LZ filename), so frame-to-frame signal change no longer tracks FD in any
  subject, matched or not. This test is uninformative on these data; it is not evidence against alignment.

## Verdict (plain, per file pair)
  timeseries <-> FD:      VERIFIED at one subject (co-located defect at subject index 2, PCB, TR 839; C),
                          supported by the layout in the MATLAB (A); weak statistical support (E: TDMI vs FD on
                          PCB p ~ 0.04, others null). A single co-located defect fixes one row; it does not by
                          itself exclude a permutation of the other 13, which the E tests do not have power to detect.
  timeseries <-> EEG LZ:  VERIFIED statistically (E: sts and TDMI vs LZ on DMT, matched clearly more negative than
                          mismatched, p < 0.01, 20,000 permutations) and by the MATLAB per-subject loop (A).
  ratings <-> timeseries: PLAUSIBLE. Asserted by the MATLAB per-subject loop (A); the ratings arrays are in
                          ascending subject-code order (D); no statistical test discriminates (E: p 0.24-0.56)
                          because after removing the group-mean curve the ratings carry little subject-specific shape.
  ratings <-> FD:         PLAUSIBLE (E: p ~ 0.08, matched +0.19 vs mismatched +0.01, in the expected direction).
  Overall: alignment VERIFIED for timeseries-FD (one subject) and timeseries-LZ; PLAUSIBLE, not verified, for
  the ratings. Nothing found contradicts a single shared ordering. No file carries subject IDs on the
  numeric arrays except the ratings tables, so a full verification would need the authors' subject list.

